          %h3#baryon_chiral_perturbation_theory Baryon Chiral Perturbation Theory
          %p
            The QCD vacuum breaks chiral symmetry down to the diagonal subgroup of SU(3),
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(3)_v
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goldstone+boson"} goldstone bosons
            appear, each of which are coupled via
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0
            to the conserved axial-vector current. The physics of these
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goldstone+boson"} goldstone bosons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+perturbation+theory"} chiral perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            in the expansion of the physical vacuum state of QCD, one can write the effective theory in terms of baryon fields with a definite velocity,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_v
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi L
                    %mi v
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mi iTr
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo *
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi v
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mi DTr
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi v
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi v
                    %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mi FTr
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi v
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi v
                    %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 4
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mi Tr
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi μ
                  %mi Σ
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi μ
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi aTrM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
